    <h1 class="article-title">Business Analytics Tools Used by Uber, Netflix and Airbnb</h1>
    <p class="article-dek">What do Uber, Netflix, and Airbnb use to turn data into decisions? A plain-English tour of their analytics stacks, and the small-business version you can build.</p>

    <div class="article-byline">
      <div class="article-avatar" style="background:#2E3A55;">LI</div>
      <div><div class="article-author">Lindsay</div><div class="article-role">Consultant, Tycoon Clap</div></div>
      <div class="article-time">9 min read</div>
    </div>

    <div class="article-hero-art"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 160" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="28" y="20" width="184" height="120" rx="14" fill="#DCEFE9"/><rect x="40" y="36" width="44" height="26" rx="6" fill="#2E3A55"/><rect x="98" y="36" width="44" height="26" rx="6" fill="#E9584C"/><rect x="156" y="36" width="44" height="26" rx="6" fill="#357566"/><path d="M62 62v14h58M120 62v14M178 62v14h-58" stroke="#9FB6AE" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"/><rect x="82" y="76" width="76" height="22" rx="8" fill="#F3B94D"/><path d="M120 98v10" stroke="#9FB6AE" stroke-width="2.5"/><rect x="48" y="108" width="144" height="22" rx="8" fill="#fff"/><rect x="58" y="118" width="12" height="8" rx="2" fill="#4C9C8B"/><rect x="76" y="114" width="12" height="12" rx="2" fill="#357566"/><rect x="94" y="116" width="12" height="10" rx="2" fill="#2E3A55"/><path d="M118 122l14-6 12 4 16-8" stroke="#E9584C" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg></div>

    <div class="article-grid">
      <article class="article-content" id="articleContent">
        <p>When people search for <strong>business analytics tools</strong>, they usually want one thing: to know what the best companies actually use, so they can copy the useful parts. Uber, Netflix, and Airbnb are good examples because all three have written publicly about their data platforms, and several of the tools they built are now open source and used by thousands of other companies.</p>
        <p>This guide walks through what each company has said about its analytics stack, what those tools do in plain English, and what a small or mid-sized business can borrow without needing a hundred engineers. A note on dates: the tool details below come from each company's own published posts, some of which are several years old. Stacks evolve, so treat this as a map of ideas rather than a current inventory.</p>

        <h2 id="what-is-business-analytics">Business analytics vs. business intelligence, in one minute</h2>
        <p><strong>What is business intelligence?</strong> It's the practice of turning raw <strong>business information data</strong> into reports and dashboards that show what's happening in the company right now. <strong>Business analytics</strong> goes a step further: it explains why something happened and uses forecasting and machine learning to estimate what will happen next. A <strong>business intelligence analyst</strong> sits between the two, shaping data into something decision-makers can read at a glance.</p>
        <p>Behind every good <strong>business dashboard</strong> is a chain of tools: something to collect data, somewhere to store it, something to clean and model it, and something to display it. The three companies below built that chain at huge scale, but the links are the same ones a ten-person company needs.</p>

        <h2 id="uber">Uber: a data lake, fast SQL, and an ML platform</h2>
        <p>In a 2017 engineering post on analytics, Uber described a platform built around a Hadoop-based data lake. Raw data was ingested into HDFS, with Hive and Spark handling batch and ETL jobs. The post also described Kafka replicating event logs into the warehouse, Sqoop copying tables from operational databases, and Uber's own Hoodie library handling incremental updates, which kept raw-table ingestion latency to roughly 30 minutes.</p>
        <p>The layer analysts actually touched was <strong>Presto</strong>, an open-source distributed SQL engine. Uber said its Presto cluster had grown beyond 300 nodes, could reach over five petabytes of data, and finished more than 90 percent of queries within 60 seconds. By late 2016, more than two thousand people were running over 100,000 analytic queries a day. For real-time analysis, the same post mentioned Flink, Pinot, and MemSQL.</p>
        <p>On top of that foundation, Uber built <strong>Michelangelo</strong>, an internal machine learning platform described in 2017. It covers the whole lifecycle: managing data, training, evaluating, deploying, making predictions, and monitoring them. Uber reported around 10,000 features in a shared feature store and top models serving more than 250,000 predictions per second. Its worked example was estimated delivery time for UberEATS.</p>
        <p><strong>The business lesson:</strong> Uber separated storage, fast querying, and prediction into distinct layers, so thousands of people could ask questions without slowing each other down.</p>

        <h2 id="netflix">Netflix: open table formats and data science on the cloud</h2>
        <p>Netflix is best known in the data world for two contributions. <strong>Apache Iceberg</strong> was started at Netflix by Ryan Blue and Dan Weeks and donated to the Apache Software Foundation in November 2018. It's a table format that lets multiple engines, such as Spark and Hive, work safely on the same big-data tables at the same time. In practical terms, it makes huge datasets behave like reliable SQL tables.</p>
        <p>The second is <strong>Metaflow</strong>, a Python framework that Netflix's ML infrastructure team open-sourced in December 2019 with AWS. It helps data scientists structure their work so it runs in the cloud without becoming infrastructure experts. According to AWS, it uses Amazon S3 for workflow state, AWS Batch for compute, Amazon RDS for tracking runs, and integrates with SageMaker notebooks, Step Functions, and CloudWatch.</p>
        <div class="article-pull">"The best analytics stacks aren't the most complicated ones. They make the right answer the easy answer."</div>
        <p><strong>The business lesson:</strong> Netflix invested in tools that remove friction for the people doing analysis, so more time goes to questions and less to plumbing.</p>

        <h2 id="airbnb">Airbnb: pipelines, dashboards, and one version of the truth</h2>
        <p>Airbnb's contribution to everyday analytics is hard to overstate. <strong>Apache Airflow</strong>, a tool for scheduling and monitoring data pipelines, was started at Airbnb in October 2014, joined the Apache Incubator in March 2016, and became a top-level Apache project in January 2019. Another Airbnb-born project, <strong>Apache Superset</strong>, is an open-source dashboard and data exploration tool, and Airbnb's data team has been associated with it for years.</p>
        <p>Airbnb also tackled a problem almost every company has: different teams reporting different numbers for the same metric. As analyst Benn Stancil describes it, Data Science and Finance sometimes gave diverging answers because they used different tables and business logic. Airbnb's response was <strong>Minerva</strong>, a central repository for thousands of metrics that sits between raw tables and the tools people use to read them.</p>
        <p><strong>The business lesson:</strong> define each metric once, in one place, and make every dashboard read from that definition. It's the cheapest way to end arguments about whose number is right.</p>

        <h2 id="patterns">What all three have in common</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A single place for data.</strong> A data lake or warehouse that every team reads from, instead of private spreadsheets.</li>
          <li><strong>Different tools for different speeds.</strong> Batch processing for heavy jobs, fast SQL for analysts, streaming for real-time needs.</li>
          <li><strong>Shared metric definitions.</strong> One version of revenue, one version of an active customer.</li>
          <li><strong>Self-service access.</strong> Analysts and managers answer their own questions instead of waiting on a queue.</li>
          <li><strong>Open-source building blocks.</strong> Presto, Iceberg, Airflow, Superset, and Metaflow are all available to everyone.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="small-business-stack">The small-business version of the same stack</h2>
        <p>You don't need a 300-node cluster. You need the same layers at a size that fits:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Sources:</strong> your CRM, ad platforms, Shopify or Stripe, accounting, and website analytics.</li>
          <li><strong>Storage and modeling:</strong> a cloud warehouse such as BigQuery, Snowflake, or Azure, or a carefully built data model inside Power BI for smaller needs.</li>
          <li><strong>Pipelines:</strong> scheduled refreshes or a light orchestration tool, the small cousin of Airflow.</li>
          <li><strong>Dashboards:</strong> Power BI, Looker Studio, or Tableau, built around the decisions you make weekly.</li>
          <li><strong>Forecasting:</strong> simple <strong>business forecasting tools</strong>, often built into your BI platform or a spreadsheet model, before anything exotic.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If you want specifics on dashboards for a smaller team, see our guide to <a href="power-bi-consulting-for-small-business.html">Power BI consulting for small business</a> and our breakdown of <a href="power-bi-consulting-cost.html">Power BI consulting cost</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="choosing-tools">How to choose business analytics tools</h2>
        <p>Before comparing vendors, answer four questions. <strong>What decisions do we need to make faster?</strong> Tools should follow decisions, not the other way around. <strong>Where does our data live today?</strong> Pick tools that connect to your existing CRM, ad platforms, and accounting software without manual exports. <strong>Who will use the output?</strong> A founder who checks a dashboard on Monday needs something different from an analyst building models. <strong>Who will maintain it?</strong> A stack that only one consultant understands is a risk, so insist on documentation and a handover session. Price matters too, but license cost is usually smaller than the cost of the time spent building and fixing reports.</p>

        <h2 id="hiring-help">When to bring in analytics help</h2>
        <p>Companies usually hire help when reports take days to assemble, numbers don't match across teams, or leadership wants forecasts nobody has time to build. At that point you can hire a <strong>business intelligence analyst</strong>, work with a consultant, or use <strong>business analysis services</strong> from an analytics partner. When comparing <strong>business analyst companies</strong>, ask about data quality processes, reporting and handover, and what happens after launch. Tools matter less than whether the numbers are trusted.</p>

        <h2 id="our-approach">How Tycoon Clap approaches business analytics</h2>
        <p>We start from the decisions you need to make, then connect the data sources that inform them, define the metrics once, and build a small set of dashboards your team will actually open. That's the same principle Airbnb applied to thousands of metrics, scaled to your business. If you want to see what that could look like for your numbers, book a strategy call.</p>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <div class="article-faq">
          <details open><summary>What are business analytics tools?</summary><p class="faq-a">Business analytics tools collect, store, clean, analyze, and visualize business data so teams can make decisions. They typically include data pipelines, a data warehouse or lake, a BI and dashboard tool such as Power BI, and forecasting or machine learning tools.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What is the difference between business analytics and business intelligence?</summary><p class="faq-a">Business intelligence mainly describes what has happened and what is happening now, through reports and dashboards. Business analytics goes further into why it happened and what is likely to happen next, using statistics, forecasting, and machine learning. Most companies need both.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Do small businesses need the same tools as Uber or Netflix?</summary><p class="faq-a">No. Those companies built custom platforms for enormous data volumes. A small business usually needs its data sources connected, a simple warehouse or well-built data model, and a business dashboard tool such as Power BI. The principles carry over; the scale does not.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What does a business intelligence analyst do?</summary><p class="faq-a">A business intelligence analyst gathers requirements, prepares and models data, builds dashboards and reports, and helps stakeholders interpret the results. Small companies often outsource this to a consultant or an analytics partner instead of hiring full time.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How do I choose business analysis services or a business analyst company?</summary><p class="faq-a">Look for a clear scoping process, examples of dashboards in your industry, transparent pricing, and a plan for handing the work over to your team. Ask how they handle data quality, because that is where most projects succeed or fail.</p></details>
        </div><p style="font-size:.88rem;color:var(--ink-dim);line-height:1.7;margin-top:2rem;">Sources: <a href="https://www.uber.com/us/en/blog/presto/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uber Engineering: Presto and Apache Parquet (2017)</a>, <a href="https://www.uber.com/us/en/blog/michelangelo-machine-learning-platform/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uber Engineering: Michelangelo (2017)</a>, <a href="https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/2024/03/07/iceberg-at-netflix-and-beyond-with-ryan-blue/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Software Engineering Daily on Apache Iceberg</a>, <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/how-netflixs-ml-framework-metaflow-drives-open-source-adoption-with-aws-service-catalog" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS on Netflix's Metaflow</a>, <a href="https://airflow.apache.org/docs/apache-airflow/2.0.1/project.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Airflow project history</a>, <a href="https://benn.substack.com/p/minerva-metrics-layer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Benn Stancil on Airbnb's Minerva</a>. Tycoon Clap is not affiliated with Uber, Netflix, or Airbnb.</p>
